import json
from pathlib import Path
root=Path(__file__).parent
modules=[
 dict(id=1,title='Think like a data engineer',name='Data engineering foundations',outcome='CO1',color='sage',description='Understand the people, systems and lifecycle behind useful data.',chapters='Fundamentals of Data Engineering · Chapters 1–2',lessons=[
 ('The data engineering landscape','Define data engineering and distinguish engineering responsibilities from data science.', [('The core idea','Data engineering designs, builds and operates systems that turn raw data into reliable, usable data. Data science often uses that data for analysis and prediction; engineering makes the inputs accessible and dependable.'),('Roles change with context','A small team may need one generalist to own ingestion, storage and analytics. A larger organization may separate platform, analytics and infrastructure responsibilities. Data maturity describes how consistently an organization manages and uses its data.'),('Business meets engineering','Clarify the decision the data will support, who owns it and when it must arrive. Technical responsibilities include modeling, orchestration, quality, security and operations. A technically impressive pipeline has little value if it misses the business need.')], 'Choose a campus use case. List its data producers, consumers and one business question.'),
 ('Follow the data lifecycle','Trace data from generation to serving and identify lifecycle undercurrents.', [('From source to use','Operational applications, devices and external services generate data. Engineers ingest it, choose suitable storage, transform it and serve it to downstream consumers. The wider data lifecycle also includes retention and eventual deletion.'),('Batch or continuous','A daily enrollment report can use scheduled batch ingestion. A live service may need streaming updates. Select the arrival pattern from latency and correctness needs rather than tool popularity.'),('The undercurrents','Security, data management, DataOps, architecture, orchestration and software engineering span the lifecycle. For example, a change to a source schema can require updates to contracts, tests, permissions and downstream models.')], 'Draw a source → ingestion → storage → transformation → serving flow. Add quality and ownership checkpoints.'),
 ('Skills, ownership and trade-offs','Connect team maturity and responsibilities with practical engineering choices.', [('Start with responsibility','For every dataset, identify an owner, consumers, expected freshness and a recovery plan. Ownership helps teams resolve incidents without guessing who should act.'),('Build a balanced skill set','SQL, programming and data modeling support implementation. Communication, requirements analysis and prioritization connect those skills to business needs. Operational habits make the system dependable over time.'),('Assess maturity','A team using manual exports needs different improvements from a team operating governed, automated pipelines. Choose the next reliable step and document the trade-off.')], 'Write a dataset contract with its owner, schema, freshness expectation and failure response.')],quiz=('Which concern spans the entire data engineering lifecycle?', ['Security and data management','Only chart design','Only model tuning'],0,'Security and data management affect every stage, from source access to serving and retention.')),
 dict(id=2,title='Design systems that hold up',name='Data architecture & technology',outcome='CO2',color='blue',description='Make architecture decisions around reliability, coupling and business value.',chapters='Fundamentals of Data Engineering · Chapters 3–4',lessons=[
 ('Principles of good architecture','Connect enterprise goals with data architecture decisions.', [('Architecture has a purpose','Enterprise architecture connects business processes and technology. Data architecture describes how data is collected, stored, transformed and used within that larger system.'),('Design for change','Prefer clear interfaces, explicit ownership and components that can evolve without forcing every other component to change. Document the assumptions behind each choice.'),('Design for failure','Distributed components can fail independently. Plan retries, timeouts, idempotent writes and recovery paths. Reliability is a design concern, not only an operations concern.')], 'Sketch an architecture for attendance analytics. Annotate one failure mode and its recovery path.'),
 ('Coupling, services and scale','Compare monoliths, tiers and microservices in a data system.', [('Coupling creates obligations','Tightly coupled components depend on each other\'s implementation or availability. Loose coupling uses stable interfaces and can reduce the impact of change, but often adds operational complexity.'),('Pick a sensible boundary','A monolith may be easier for a small team to build and operate. Services allow independent development and scaling when boundaries and ownership are clear. Neither structure is universally best.'),('Scaling is multidimensional','Consider data volume, request rates, latency and the number of independent teams. Partitioning and asynchronous communication help some workloads but introduce consistency and coordination trade-offs.')], 'Compare a shared-database monolith with event-connected services using cost, reliability and team effort.'),
 ('Choose technology deliberately','Evaluate technology choices using team capability, cost and interoperability.', [('Fit before fashion','Evaluate team size, skills, delivery speed, operational burden and compatibility with existing systems. A tool the team can operate well may outperform a more fashionable alternative.'),('Look beyond the bill','Total cost of ownership includes development, maintenance, training, data transfer and incident response. Tie spending to the business outcome and expected service levels.'),('Cloud choices','Hybrid cloud combines local and cloud environments; multicloud uses more than one cloud provider. Both can address constraints but add integration and governance work. Distinguish durable architectural principles from rapidly changing tools.')], 'Create a decision record comparing two storage options. State assumptions and a condition that would change your decision.')],quiz=('What belongs in total cost of ownership?', ['Only the monthly service invoice','Development, operations and training as well as service costs','Only initial setup time'],1,'Total cost of ownership accounts for the effort and expense of operating the system over its life.')),
 dict(id=3,title='Make machine learning repeatable',name='MLOps & model development',outcome='CO3',color='orange',description='Move from a promising experiment to a reproducible, risk-aware workflow.',chapters='Introducing MLOps · Chapters 1–2',lessons=[
 ('Why MLOps exists','Identify model lifecycle risks and the role of MLOps.', [('More than a trained model','A useful ML system needs data, code, runtime environments, evaluation and operational ownership. A good offline score alone does not establish production readiness.'),('Recognize risk','Risks include unreliable inputs, irreproducible experiments, inappropriate use and performance changes after deployment. Assess likelihood and impact, then define controls and owners.'),('Responsible development','Document intended use and limitations. Examine error patterns across relevant groups and use cases. Decide when a person must review a result or stop a deployment.')], 'Make a risk register for a house-price model with three risks, their impacts and proposed controls.'),
 ('From objective to features','Connect business objectives, exploration and feature selection.', [('Define success first','Translate a business question into a prediction target, an evaluation metric and an acceptable error range. Include a simple baseline so a complex model must justify its added effort.'),('Explore before training','Inspect missing values, outliers, distributions, time boundaries and target quality. Split data to reflect how future predictions will be made; keep evaluation data out of feature selection.'),('Engineer with care','Encoding, scaling, aggregation and domain-derived features can help. Avoid target leakage: a feature unavailable at prediction time can create misleading evaluation results.')], 'For churn prediction, propose three features available before the prediction date and one leakage feature to exclude.'),
 ('Reproducible training & evaluation','Record the inputs and artifacts required to repeat an experiment.', [('An experiment is a record','Track dataset version, code revision, dependency versions, parameters, random seeds and metrics. Store the resulting model artifact with the experiment that produced it.'),('Compare appropriate baselines','Select algorithms for the target, data and operational constraints. Compare with consistent splits and metrics. Accuracy alone may hide important error patterns on imbalanced data.'),('Reproduction has limits','Seeds help but may not guarantee bit-for-bit results across hardware and libraries. Document expected variability and verify that the relevant conclusions remain stable.')], 'Design an experiment log that another student could use to reproduce your baseline.')],quiz=('Which practice best supports reproducibility?', ['Saving only the final accuracy','Recording data, code, environment and parameters','Renaming the model file each time'],1,'Reproducing a run requires its inputs and environment, not just its final score.')),
 dict(id=4,title='Ship with confidence',name='Production & CI/CD',outcome='CO4',color='sage',description='Validate artifacts, automate delivery and plan a safe production release.',chapters='Introducing MLOps · Chapters 3–4',lessons=[
 ('Prepare a production runtime','Explain environment differences and model validation before launch.', [('Development is not production','Production has different traffic, permissions, dependencies and reliability expectations. Package the model and its runtime so behavior is consistent and configuration is explicit.'),('Validate access and behavior','Check input schemas, data access, dependencies and prediction interfaces before launch. Separate validation from the training process and test against the intended use.'),('Evaluate model risk','A higher-impact use case may need more review, documentation and monitoring. Decide release criteria before evaluating the candidate model, and record approval decisions.')], 'Write a pre-launch checklist covering environment, access, data schema and model behavior.'),
 ('Build the CI/CD pipeline','Design a delivery pipeline with meaningful quality gates.', [('Continuous integration','A code change triggers checks such as unit tests, schema validation and data-quality checks. Versioned artifacts connect each result to the source and environment that produced it.'),('From candidate to release','A pipeline can train, evaluate, package and validate a model before promoting it. Model metrics are one gate alongside security, interface and operational checks.'),('Automate with judgment','Continuous delivery keeps a validated candidate ready to release. Continuous deployment releases automatically after gates pass. Human approval may be appropriate depending on risk.')], 'Draw commit → test → train → evaluate → package → stage → approve → deploy. Add a stop condition to three stages.'),
 ('Deployment & maintenance','Compare deployment modes and plan rollback and maintenance.', [('Match the serving pattern','Batch prediction produces results on a schedule. Online prediction responds to requests. Select the mode using latency, throughput and business needs.'),('Release gradually','A canary release sends a small share of traffic to a new version. Blue-green uses parallel environments and a traffic switch. Shadow deployment evaluates a candidate without using its predictions for live decisions.'),('Keep a recovery path','Retain the last validated model and compatible environment. Monitor service health and model behavior, define rollback triggers and assign someone to respond.')], 'Plan a canary release. Define the traffic share, evaluation window, success criteria and rollback trigger.')],quiz=('What is the purpose of a release gate?', ['Stop every release permanently','Require agreed checks before promotion','Replace monitoring after deployment'],1,'Release gates prevent promotion until explicit quality and risk criteria are met.')),
 dict(id=5,title='Keep models trustworthy',name='Monitoring & governance',outcome='CO5',color='blue',description='Detect change, close the feedback loop and make model decisions traceable.',chapters='Introducing MLOps · Chapters 5–6',lessons=[
 ('Recognize model degradation','Distinguish input drift from changes in model performance.', [('The world changes','A model can degrade as customer behavior, products or data collection change. Monitor service health, input data and outcomes because each reveals a different class of problem.'),('Drift is a signal','Input drift means a feature distribution has changed relative to a reference. It does not prove prediction quality has declined. Investigate the cause and evaluate outcomes when labels are available.'),('Use ground truth','Ground-truth evaluation compares predictions with observed outcomes. Labels may arrive late, so define the evaluation window and distinguish early proxy signals from confirmed performance changes.')], 'For a demand forecast, list one service metric, one input-drift signal and one ground-truth metric.'),
 ('Build the feedback loop','Design monitoring and retraining decisions with validation.', [('Choose a reference','Compare recent inputs to a documented baseline. Missingness rates, summary statistics and distribution comparisons can highlight change; thresholds should reflect the use case and data variability.'),('Investigate before reacting','A drift alert could indicate real population change or a broken upstream transformation. Check data quality and context before retraining a model on potentially corrupted inputs.'),('Retraining is another release','Trigger retraining based on evidence or a schedule, then apply the same evaluation and release gates used for initial deployment. Keep an audit record and a rollback option.')], 'Write an alert playbook: trigger, owner, checks, decision and recovery. Include a reason not to retrain immediately.'),
 ('Govern the model lifecycle','Connect documentation and controls with model risk.', [('Make ownership visible','A model inventory records purpose, owner, versions, status and intended users. A model card adds evaluation results, data limitations and known failure modes.'),('Scale controls with risk','Use review, access control, lineage, approvals and change logs in proportion to the impact of model decisions. Reproducibility and traceability support responsible operations.'),('Regulatory context','Model governance includes risk management, privacy-aware data handling and clear accountability. Study how documentation, data handling and accountability relate to governance. Actual obligations depend on jurisdiction and context; this lesson is a conceptual introduction.')], 'Draft a model card with intended use, excluded uses, training data summary, evaluation, limitations and owner.')],quiz=('An input-drift alert means…', ['The model must be retrained immediately','The input distribution changed and needs investigation','The model is certainly inaccurate'],1,'Drift identifies a change in inputs; investigate data quality and outcome performance before deciding how to respond.'))
]
for m in modules:
 m['lessons']=[dict(id=f"m{m['id']}l{i+1}",title=l[0],objective=l[1],sections=[dict(title=s[0],body=s[1]) for s in l[2]],activity=l[3]) for i,l in enumerate(m['lessons'])]
 q=m['quiz'];m['quiz']=dict(question=q[0],options=q[1],answer=q[2],explanation=q[3])
